>correct:  "The goal is maximization of total profit on on the capital
>invested in the venture,"

 There is good reason to believe that a truly singleminded pursuit
of profit is less effective at maximizing profit than a singleminded
pursuit of customer satisfaction. There was a poular book written by
Tom Peters (?) about a decade ago which described some of the
differences between the most successfull corporations and ordinary
ones, which reported many examples of successfull companies doing
things to help customers that couldn't possibly be considered profit
maximizing if you were to calculate the results of the individual
actions. The point is that trying to calculate what is profitable
directly is impractical, because most people will overestimate
readily quantifiable contributors to profits, and underestimate
intagibles such as reputation.

 I seem to recall Texas Air's stock (Eastern's parent) going from
a peak of 40 to something like 5 under Lorenzo.

 It is foolish to expect that corporate employees will always
maximize the company's profits. Shareholders set up incentives
to encourage profit-maximizing behavior, but employees have other
goals and often get away with pursuing those. The corporate structure
is merely better than the alternatives at producing profits.
