Date: 22 Jan 93 00:36:36 GMT
From: ssandfort@attmail.com
Subject: META--The Great Debate

SSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSSS

I have to confess.  I think I enjoy "Geraldo," "A Current Affair"
and "Hard Copy" just a little TOO much.  Sensationalism can be
entertaining, but it's not very informative.

Similarly, I'm often amused by the sarcasm, name-calling and
general flaming, that occasionally occurs on this channel.  When
I stop to think about it, though, I signed up on this list for
something more than intellectual mud wrestling.  I would much
prefer to see some resolution (or at least closure) regarding the
topics discussed in this forum.

One of the problems I see with the exchanged that occur here is
that they are too unstructured.  Participants are quick to jump
on each other's weak points, but rarely do they acknowledge
strong ones--they just ignore them.  That's easy to understand.
The degree of ego involvement here is high.  When one gets
slam-dunked, it hurts.  Admitting it, hurts even more.  While
this may be understandable, it is a disservice to the virtual
audience.  Inquiring minds want to know.

ONE POSSIBLE SOLUTION--Let's have a formal debate mechanism for
topics which generate a lot of heat.  (I can just see Perry
chomping at the bit to challenge David Jevins.)  If we construct
our format well, it won't be easy for the debaters to ignore
valid points and questions posed by their opponents.

When the debate has run its course, we could have a poll to pick
a "winner."  If we really wanted to make things interesting, the
parties to the debate could bet something on the outcome.  This
could reasonably lead to "put up, or shut up" challenges to
obnoxious, opinionated posters.  A failure to accept a debate
challenge might have serious "reputation" consequences.

If you think this idea sucks, skip the rest of this post and/or
tell me why you think so (publicly or privately--I don't care
which).  If you think debates have a place here, then read on.  I
have included suggestions on possible format/rules we might wish
to adopt for such contests.

In any event, I've put considerable time into this suggestion.
Please let me know what you think.


                        THE GREAT DEBATE
                          Ground Rules


THE PROPOSITION--In formal debate, the topic to be discussed is
expressed as a proposition of the form, "RESOLVED that . . ."
followed by a call for a change to the status quo.  It may also
assert a belief which is not generally thought to be true.  For
Extropian purposes, the "status quo" includes current scientific
teachings plus their reasonable extrapolations.  Syntactically,
the proposition should not be worded against something (e.g.,
RESOLVED: that modern corporations are inefficient and inhumane).
Instead, the proposition must stand FOR something (e.g.,
RESOLVED:  that syndicalism is a more efficient and humane form
of business organization).

AFFIRMATIVE/NEGATIVE--The "Affirmative" side of a debate argues
in favor of the proposition.  The "Negative" defends the status
quo against the proposition.  In formal debate, the Negative my
oppose both the proposition by AND the status quo by offering a
"counter-plan."  While this may be an effective tactic in formal
debate, it makes the issues less clear-cut.  Extropian debates
will use only the "plan-vs-status-quo" format.

TIME AND TURNS--In a formal debate, the two sides take turns
giving speeches.  Each speech has a strict time limit.  In the
context of a text-based presentation, time (or length) limits are
not necessary.  The NUMBER of times, however, each side "comes up
to bat" should be limited.  Otherwise, we will be subject to
"Forever Wars" between opponents who each want to have the "last
word" on every issue.  Extropian list debates will have five
"rounds" alternating between the sides in the pattern A-N-A-N-A.
That's right, the Affirmative gets the first and last word.  This
is because it is the Affirmative which has the "burden of
proof"--it has to PROVE its case against the status quo.  The
Negative should win even if it says NOTHING, if the Affirmative
fails to carry the burden of proof.

PARAGRAPH NOTATION FORMAT--Since our debates are in the form of
text, we can use a paragraph numbering system.  By numbering each
paragraph, we can reference them by simple citation instead of
tediously repeating previous arguments.  Also, with numbered
paragraphs it is harder to "ignore" arguments presented by the
other side.  When the debaters post their arguments, each of
their paragraphs will be preceded by "A" or "N" (Affirmative or
Negative) and a sequentially higher number (e.g., A1, A2, . . .
for the Affirmative; N1, N2, N3 . . . for the Negative).

CROSS-EXAMINATION--At the ends of the posts in rounds two and
three, specific questions could be asked of the opposing debater.
These questions would have to be answered in rounds four and
five, respectively.  The questions, like the paragraphs would be
numbered.  The numbering system would be N?1, N?2, N?3, etc.

THE POLL--If either of the parties requests it, a poll may be
taken to determine the "winner" of the debate.  Such a poll would
have two questions to answer, (1) Which debater is "right" (i.e.,
"with whom do I agree"), and (2) Which debater was "best" (i.e.,
who presented the best arguments and made the best case for their
position).  To illustrate this distinction, consider a debate
over the shape of the earth.  It is easy to make a prima facie
case that the world is flat.  If the Negative said only that the
"flat-earther" was nuts, the Negative would be "right" but the
Affirmative could still be the "best" debater of the two.

FINALITY--After a debate is concluded, the participants MAY NOT
publicly post on the topic for one month.  Other list members,
however, are free to comment on any aspect of the topic or the
debate.  (This could include another debate on the same topic,
but between different debaters.)


