Actually, that's fairly simple to deal with (in principle; the details
are messy) -- the limit of their power over you (and of your power
over them) is "what you can get elsewhere"; what each of you could get
if you walked away from the situation.  In union/managment situations,
this is governed by the job market, and leaves a relatively small zone
in which the negotiated settlement *has* to end up.

Interestingly, from what I'm able to gather from the newspaper
accounts, it was Lorenzo who was attempting to get more power over the
other side than the labor market would back up.  Not surprisingly, it
was the unions that walked out of the negotiaton, by refusing to agree
to any settlement that left Lorenzo in control.

