Date: Thu, 21 Jan 93 17:28:18 PST
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: Re: META--The Great Debate

Sandy Sandfort has written a long proposal for formally debating
controversial issues. I won't quote it for two reasons. First, its
length. Second, quoting-and-responding is the main way posts become
negative or overly critical, as Sandy noted.

Speaking for myself, I don't have the time or inclination to engage in
a formal, multi-round debate. I write my little articles and essays in
the spirit of "take it for what it's worth," not with the belief that
I will "win" and argument or, say, convert someone from one belief
system to another.

The really controversial issues on this list, such as aspects of
statism vs. aspects of capitalism, are not likely to be resolved
through any debate I can imagine people actually wading through, let
alone writing in the first place! Ditto for the various controversies
about helping the poor and clueless, the ethics of medical
experimentation, etc.

The good part of Sandy's proposal is the idea that issues get
resolved, that some consensus gets reached. The bad part is that huge
amounts of time are needed to learn about issues, to listen to debate,
to write the debate in the first place (pity those chosen to argue
certain postions!), and to ultimately vote on the outcome.

And what does a vote mean? If 70% of the list decides that the
anarchocapitalist view of commerce is correct, does this mean it is?
How about if 55% believe the Nanotech Rapture is coming in 15 years?

So what's my solution?

View this list as a small subset of the universe of discourse seen
with books, articles, papers, and ideas in general. We don't expect
voting of the population to decide, for example, whether "The
Machinery of Freedom" is superior to "The Greening of America," do we?

I still think the list will have increasing problems with its current
structure if membership increases. But I doubt that voting will bring
order to chaos.

But I applaud Sandy for thinking about the issues. Let's vote on it!


-Tim May




