proposed a formal format for debating "hot" issues.  I think,
though, that I may not have made myself completely clear as to
how debates would arise, and our purpose in having them.

Probably, MOST list members have similar inclinations to those of
Tim.  That is, they lack the time or inclination to research and
write formal debate arguments.  But most is not all.  As it
stands, there are a number of posters who devote considerable
time to "informal" debate.  From their ranks would come the
debaters.

The only people "chosen" to debate would be self-selected.  As I
suggested in my posting, I can easily imagine Perry Metzger
issuing a debate challenge to any number of newbees on the list.
(Maybe even me.  I can just see it, "RESOLVED, that formal
debating on the Extropian list is a dumb idea.")  If they picked
up the gauntlet, we would have ourselves a debate.  If not, well,
"business as usual" I guess.

What would be the purpose of declaring a "winner"?  Not to
ultimately decide what is Truth, Justice and the American Way, I
assure you.  Nor do I think anybody's mind would be changed.  No,
my proposal has a "hidden agenda" of four purposes.  FIRST, to
let intellectual opponents "check their premises" in the harsh
light of peer scrutiny.  SECOND, to encourage antagonists to
fairly and politely consider each other's points of view.  THIRD,
to set a limit on the amount of bandwidth devoted to interminable
"is/is-not" arguments.  FOURTH, to provide the lurkers with more
light and less heat on controversial topics. The only thing
voting would accomplish would be to let the debaters know how
well their peers thought they did.  Kind of like Ed Koch asking,
"How'm I doin'?"

Do I think this list will/should be converted to a debating
society?  Heaven forefend!  I DO think it would add another
communication tool to the Extropian bag of tricks.  THAT'S what
I'm really proposing.  Other comments?

