Date: Thu, 21 Jan 93 19:22:16 -0800
From: David Jevans <jevans@apple.com>
Subject: Re:  CORP: shareholder interests

> Exactly. One must, in order to maximize profit, produce good products, keep
> customers happy, and prevent valued human resources from leaving due
> to dissatisfaction.

There is another scenario where this isn't required to maximize profit:
namely, monopoly.

What about establishing a monomoply from which no customer can escape and
against which no company can plausibly launch an attack?  Then you have
no real reason to keep improving your product, you just rest on your
laurels, reaping the profits.

A crude example might be the PC industry, where it will be virtually
impossible to convert existing customers to a different platform, due to
their huge investment in the platform over time (application programs,
peripherals, databases). Once you have everyone, and of course new customers
must buy your stuff because they have to be compatible with it, then you just
slack off on the development, and just trickle out improvements at a rate
fast enough to keep people constantly upgrading (continuous revenue stream),
yet you don't have to bother putting work into creating much better products.
After a certain number of years and incremental growth, no startup will be
able to invest the kind of money and time it would take to become even
remotely competetive, nevermind actually create a product that is compellingly
better to woo over your customers.

This is already evident to some extent in today's PC market.

Another example is when there are limited resources, like tropical islands
or diamond mines, and you get a monopoly.  Noone can compete, yet they
have no need to improve the "product" (eg. hotels on islands, or supply
of diamonds).

These seem like great schemes to maximize profit without bothering to make
a good product (more exactly, without having to improve the product), without
having to care one whit about the customers, and, because you have very little
R&D, you don't care about most of your employees, and in fact want them
to quit!  More profit!

This time, please don't read some crazy notion into this that I'm against
capitalism, corporations, or that I like anti-trust laws.  I'm just proposing
a possible situation.

