From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: Nutrition

> From: jrk@information-systems.east-anglia.ac.uk (Richard Kennaway)
> 
> My own somewhat sceptical view of diets, supplements, and medical advice
> about nutrition, is that I eat and drink whatever I want.  When I see some
> new dietary scare I just think "Look what they're telling us now" and
> dismiss it. 

I believe the advice has been consistant for many years now. At the very
least, eat a low fat diet with a decent supply of fiber. The links between
high fat intake and heart disease, stroke, several kinds of cancers (including
colon cancer) and other fairly bad things like Type II diabetes are
extremely well established at this point.

In fact, I'd argue (although others would differ a bit on this point) that
the studies thus far have shown that if you want to lead a disease free
life, in the long run you should follow a low fat vegan diet, such as
the one Dr. Dean Ornish's patients followed in his ground breaking study.
For those not in the know, Ornish's patients following this diet actually
reversed their heart disease, which is pretty incredible if you know anything
about the success rates of the other available therapies. There are many
other advantages I can name to veganism, such as reduced protein intake.
Most westerners eat far far too much protein, the metabolism of which has
been strongly linked to, at the very least, kidney disease and osteoporosis.

In short, I'd say that at the very least you should not ignore the advice
about diet completely. Even if veganism is a bit contraversial, lowering
your intake of fat and trying to eat a balanced diet is now almost certainly
demonstrated to be a good thing.

If you completely ignore diet you are not guaranteed an early grave, but you
are certainly assured of a much higher probability of one. Given the state
of cryonics these days, I'd rather not be suspended unless absolutely
unavoidable.

Perry


